window.SEARCH_INDEX = [
  {t:'Glossary — International Logistics', u:'glossary.html', text:'Home → Glossary Glossary of terms Key terms of international logistics, sea transport and chartering. Freight-correspondence abbreviations are collected separately — in the section "Chartering abbreviations" . Term Definition Adjustment (ADJ) A correction of the freight rate depending on changing parameters (parcel size, season, conditions). Used in long-term contracts. Chartering The transfer of a vessel by the shipowner into the temporary possession or use of the charterer. See also "Bareboat charter", "Time charter", "Voyage charter". Delivery basis (Incoterms) Delivery terms that define the point at which risk and costs pass from seller to buyer. See the "Incoterms 2020" section for details. Barter An exchange of goods without monetary settlement. Rarely used in sea transport. Bareboat / Demise Charter A charter of the vessel without crew: the charterer takes the vessel into full possession and control, mans it itself and bears all operating costs. Broker (ship / chartering) An intermediary between shipowner and charterer in concluding a chartering deal. Can be exclusive (under a framework agreement) or market (on a per-order basis). Additional Charges Costs of loading, discharging and handling cargo on top of the base freight. Guarantor A person liable for performance of obligations if the principal debtor is insufficient. General Agency (GA) A representation of a shipping company in ports, servicing its vessels. General Average Losses incurred through deliberate and reasonable expenditure or sacrifice for the common safety of the vessel and cargo; apportioned between all participants in the voyage in proportion to value. Deadweight (DWT) The full carrying capacity of a vessel — the total weight of cargo, fuel, stores and crew that the vessel can take at its load-line draught. Deviation A departure of the vessel from the contractual route. Unjustified deviation may deprive the carrier of the right to limit liability. Demurrage A charge for the vessel\'s delay beyond the agreed laytime during loading or discharging. Dispatch / Despatch A premium paid to the charterer for completing cargo operations ahead of the laytime. Usually 50% of the demurrage rate. Additional Freight A surcharge on freight for special conditions (loading at an extra port, etc.). Principal / Customer A legal entity ordering carriage — the charterer or shipper. Endorsement A transfer inscription on the back of the bill of lading by which an order bill of lading is transferred to another party. Cabotage Carriage of goods and passengers between ports of one state. As a rule, permitted only to vessels under the national flag. Cancelling date The latest date by which the vessel must be tendered for loading. If the vessel has not arrived by that date, the charterer may cancel the contract. Commercial Agent (CA) A representative of the shipowner on commercial matters, negotiating freight. Confident A trusted person; an intermediate intermediary in informal negotiations. Bill of Lading (B/L) The main document of sea carriage: a receipt for the goods, evidence of the contract of carriage and a document of title to the goods. Laytime / Lay days The agreed period for loading or discharging the vessel, included in the freight. Once it expires, demurrage begins. The standard modern English term is laytime ; "lay days" is the historical form, and in modern usage "laydays" usually means the start date of the window (in the laycan pair). Liner service Regular carriage on a fixed schedule and route with published tariffs. Lump sum freight A fixed amount of freight for the whole vessel or voyage regardless of the quantity of cargo actually loaded. Dead freight A charge the charterer must pay for cargo space it reserved but did not tender. Mobilization The positioning of the vessel to the loading place under a voyage charter. Multimodal transport Carriage of goods by several modes of transport under a single document on the responsibility of one operator. Sea-air Combined carriage using sea and air transport — combining the low cost of sea and the speed of air. Night Surcharge An additional charge for cargo operations at night. Notice of Readiness (NOR) A written notice by the master that the vessel is ready to load/discharge. The laytime count usually starts from its tender. Encumbered vessel A vessel encumbered by a mortgage in connection with financing its construction or purchase. Offer / Firm offer A firm proposal to conclude a chartering deal on the stated terms, binding on the offeror for the duration of its validity. Particular Average Losses that do not qualify as general average; borne by the party that suffered them, or its insurer. Sub-charter The transfer of the vessel to a third party (sub-charter) under a voyage or time charter. Port charges Fees for the use of port facilities and services, included in the cost of carriage. Advanced Freight Payment of freight (in full or in part) before delivery of the cargo. Recap / Fixture recap A written confirmation of the main terms of a concluded chartering deal, exchanged by the parties before signing the full charter party. Recap Correction A clarification of the terms after the recap when errors are found. Voyage Charter A charter of the vessel for one or several voyages; the shipowner bears operating costs, freight is paid for carrying the cargo. Seasonal Rate A freight rate depending on the season (summer/winter period). Certificate of Registry A document certifying the vessel\'s registration and national affiliation. Special Class A vessel class for carrying heavy/special cargoes. Urgent Voyage A voyage of an urgent nature, for which a speed premium is provided. Standard Terms Unified terms of a charter party — GENCON, NYPE, BALTIME and others. Stevedore A company or person performing loading/unloading work on the vessel and in port. Shipowner / Owner The party to a charter party that provides the vessel for carriage and bears its operating costs (under a voyage charter). Surveyor An independent expert who inspects cargo and vessel, determining the quantity, condition and causes of cargo damage. Time Charter A charter of the vessel for a defined period (months, years) with crew; the charterer pays fuel and port charges, the shipowner pays crew and vessel upkeep. Time Charter Equivalent (TCE) The shipowner\'s daily revenue from operating the vessel, net of variable costs (fuel, port charges). Used to compare voyage performance. Tallyman A cargo counter who keeps a tally of packages during loading and discharging. Tonnage A vessel\'s capacity expressed in register tons; used to calculate charges. Tramp shipping Irregular carriage without a schedule at freely agreed market rates. Fixture The fact of concluding a chartering deal, when the parties have confirmed all terms (clear fixture). Freight The charge for carrying cargo by sea, set by the charter party. Ad valorem freight Freight calculated as a percentage of the value of the cargo carried. Charterer The party to a charter party that hires the vessel to carry cargo. Forwarder An organiser of cargo delivery acting as intermediary between the cargo owner and carriers, not owning transport. House broker An in-house chartering department of a large company (oil, timber, etc.) working without external intermediaries. Home Port The port of the vessel\'s registration, determining its jurisdiction. Charter Party (C/P) The contract for chartering a vessel between shipowner and charterer. Drawn up on standard forms (GENCON, NYPE, BALTIME and others). Exclusive broker A broker with a framework agreement with the principal, holding the exclusive right to find partners for its fixtures. Express Rate An increased charge for urgent delivery under tight deadlines. TEU Twenty-foot Equivalent Unit — a conventional unit equal to the volume of a standard 20-foot container. FEU Forty-foot Equivalent Unit — a conventional unit equal to the volume of a standard 40-foot container (2 TEU). GRT / NRT Gross / net register tonnage — volumetric units of a vessel (1 register ton = 100 cubic feet).'},
  {t:'Incoterms 2020 — all 11 delivery terms', u:'incoterms.html', text:'Home → Incoterms 2020 Incoterms 2020 Incoterms (International Commercial Terms) are international rules for interpreting trade terms that define which party (seller or buyer) bears the costs and risks at each stage of delivery. The current edition — Incoterms 2020 — came into force on 1 January 2020. It is important to understand: Incoterms govern the transfer of risk and allocation of costs , but they do NOT determine the transfer of ownership and do NOT replace the contract of sale. 11 terms — two classes For any mode of transport (7 terms) Term Full name Meaning EXW Ex Works The seller only makes the goods available at its own premises. The buyer bears maximum obligations. FCA Free Carrier The seller delivers the goods to the carrier nominated by the buyer at the agreed place. CPT Carriage Paid To The seller pays carriage to the destination; risk passes when the goods are handed to the first carrier. CIP Carriage and Insurance Paid To Like CPT, but the seller also insures the goods (ICC (A) level of cover). DAP Delivered at Place Delivered at destination. The seller delivers to the agreed place; the buyer unloads. DPU Delivered at Place Unloaded Delivered and unloaded. The only term where the seller must unload the goods. DDP Delivered Duty Paid Delivered with duties paid. The seller bears maximum obligations, including import clearance. For sea and inland waterway transport only (4 terms) Term Full name Meaning FAS Free Alongside Ship Free alongside ship. The seller delivers the goods alongside the vessel at the port of shipment. FOB Free On Board Free on board. Risk passes when the goods are loaded on board the vessel. CFR Cost and Freight Cost and freight. The seller pays freight to the port of destination; risk passes on loading on board. CIF Cost, Insurance and Freight Like CFR, but the seller also insures the goods (minimum cover ICC (C)). The key logic of choosing a term The closer a term is to the start of the alphabet (E, F) — the more obligations the buyer has. The closer to the end (C, D) — the more obligations the seller has. E/F-terms — the buyer arranges the main carriage. C-terms — the seller arranges and pays carriage, but risk passes early (on handing over to the carrier). D-terms — the seller bears risk and cost all the way to the destination. Practical tip: for containerised shipments, FCA is often used instead of FOB — the container is handed over to the carrier at the terminal rather than "on board the vessel". This better reflects the moment of risk transfer in modern container logistics. What else to keep in mind Under CIP (unlike CIF) insurance must be at the ICC (A) level — maximum cover. DPU is a new term in the 2020 edition (replaced DAT) and now allows delivery at any place, not only at a terminal. Incoterms are always stated together with a place : for example, "FOB Shanghai, Incoterms 2020".'},
  {t:'International Logistics and Foreign Trade — knowledge and practice', u:'index.html', text:'Incoterms 2020 All 11 delivery terms: how risks and costs are allocated between seller and buyer. 🌊 Sea freight Vessel types, bill of lading, chartering, chartering operations technique and maritime conventions. 🚂 Rail freight SMGS and consignment notes, railway conventions and rules. ✈️ Air freight Air waybill (AWB), aviation conventions and IATA rules. 🚚 Road freight CMR, consignment note, electronic consignment note, CMR and TIR conventions. Dangerous goods Classification, IATA DGR, DGD, MSDS, ADR, RID, SMGS dangerous-goods rules, emergency cards. UCP-600 Documentary credit rules: terms, types and key provisions. Glossary Dictionary of key terms in international logistics and foreign trade. 📈 Market indices — change SCFI — Shanghai Containerized Freight Index A composite container freight rate index published weekly (on Fridays) by the Shanghai Shipping Exchange. It reflects average spot rates for container shipments out of Shanghai on major routes (Europe, Mediterranean, the Americas and others). The key benchmark for export rates from China. Drewry WCI — World Container Index A composite container rate index by Drewry, published weekly (on Thursdays). It is calculated as the average rate (in USD) for a 40-foot container (FEU) across 8 key trade lanes. Widely used in index-linked freight contracts. BDI — Baltic Exchange Dry Index An index of dry-bulk freight rates published daily by the Baltic Exchange (London). It reflects rates for carrying dry bulk cargoes (ore, coal, grain) on major routes and consists of the Capesize, Panamax and Supramax sub-indices. A key indicator of the dry-bulk market. Updated: . Sources: sse.net.cn, drewry.co.uk, balticexchange.com. 📰 Logistics and shipping news Updated: . Sources: Splash 247, The Loadstar, РЖД-Партнер, SeaNews (RSS).'},
];